Authentication sources
======================

One file per source. The chain in the daemon ini names them in the order they are asked:

    [AUTH]
    CHAIN = local, pam

The driver in core/security/chain.py imports each named file and asks it in turn. The
ini account is checked against trixd.ini before the chain and is not a source. A source
knows nothing about tokens or roles: it answers who somebody is, and the daemon does the
rest.

These are not API plugins. An API plugin ships a manifest and serves routes, and lives
under trix/plugins. A source has no manifest, serves nothing, and is never loaded or
unloaded at runtime. The two words are kept apart deliberately.

Every source is a class named Source with two methods.

    authenticate(name, password)
        (True, identity)   known here and the password verified
        (False, message)   known here and refused: the chain stops, nothing else is asked
        (None, message)    not known here: the chain continues with the next source

    resolve(name)
        (True, identity)   known here, no credential checked
        (False, message)   not known here

An identity is a dict with one key:

    name         the login name as the source knows it

A source that cannot work at all, because its library is missing or its server is
unreachable, raises. The driver logs that and moves on, so a local principal still
gets in.

local       the daemon's own principal table; a row with a password digest
pam         the controller's operating system, through python-pam. On a TrinityX
            controller sssd is joined to the cluster directory, so this reaches 389ds
            with no directory settings here and no shell-out. A system account, with
            no login shell or a uid below UID_MIN, is refused
ldap        a directory bind through ldap3, settings in [AUTH_LDAP]
oidc        OpenID Connect, named in the design and not built: it answers not-known
            and the chain continues, so an ini may name it before a provider exists
